== git merge: is a cut --no-verify unique there? (merge also has --no-verify-signatures)
$ git merge --no-verif side
error: ambiguous option: no-verif (could be --no-verify-signatures or --no-verify)
usage: git merge [<options>] [<commit>...]
   or: git merge --abort
   or: git merge --continue
-> exit 129
$ git merge --no-verify side
Already up to date.
-> exit 0

== git push: prefixes that more than one option shares
$ git push --a origin
error: ambiguous option: a (could be --all or --atomic)
usage: git push [<options>] [<repository> [<refspec>...]]

    -v, --[no-]verbose    be more verbose
-> exit 129
$ git push --d origin side
error: ambiguous option: d (could be --delete or --dry-run)
usage: git push [<options>] [<repository> [<refspec>...]]

    -v, --[no-]verbose    be more verbose
-> exit 129
$ git push --de origin side
error: unable to delete 'side': remote ref does not exist
error: failed to push some refs to '../remote.git'
-> exit 1
$ git push --atomi origin feat
Everything up-to-date
-> exit 0

== git config: the newer subcommand spelling
$ git config set core.hooksPath /dev/null
-> exit 0
$ git config --get core.hooksPath
/dev/null
-> exit 0
$ git config unset core.hooksPath
-> exit 0
$ git config get core.hooksPath
-> exit 1
$ git config core.hookspath /dev/null
-> exit 0
$ git config --get core.hooksPath
/dev/null
-> exit 0
$ git config --unset core.hooksPath
-> exit 0

== husky's own switch has no effect without husky (shown for the record: the hook still runs)
$ env HUSKY=0 git commit --allow-empty -m husky-zero
HOOK-RAN
-> exit 1
